Elections Code is amended to read:"},{"action":"IS_AMENDED","code":"ELEC","text":"Section 11385 of the Elections Code is amended to read:"}],"approvedDate":"2025-10-03","chapter":{"citation":"Chapter 251, Statutes of 2025","number":"251","type":"CHP","year":"2025"},"chapteredDate":"2025-10-03","conditions":[],"contentHash":"bc64d9849d67ea6fc084188c46a1f1667c23e09c4ec3468c3f9065037c0ba0ef","digest":"(1) Existing law specifies procedures for the recall election of a local officer. Under existing law, if a majority of the votes on a recall proposal for a local officer are in the affirmative, the officer is removed and the office remains vacant until it is filled according to law.\n\nThis bill would provide that when the local officer is recalled and removed, that officer may not be appointed to fill the vacancy.\n\n(2) Existing law provides that if at a statewide recall election an officer is recalled, the candidate receiving the highest number of votes for the office shall be declared elected for the unexpired term of the recalled officer.\n\nThis bill would make clarifying changes to the above provision.","effective":{"basis":"not_verified","date":null,"note":"No effective date is recorded in the official bill data and DemocracyHQ has not verified one. A non-urgency statute enacted in a regular session generally takes effect on January 1 after a 90-day period (Cal. Const. art. IV, § 8(c)), but a bill may state other operative dates."},"expiryClauses":[],"firstObservedAt":"2026-11-20T01:12:59Z","id":"ca-20252026-ab94","identifier":"AB 94","indicators":{"Appropriation":"NO","BudgetBill":"NO","Election":"NO","FiscalCommittee":"NO","ImmediateEffect":"NO","LocalProgram":"NO","TaxLevy":"NO","Urgency":"NO","UsualCurrentExpenses":"NO","VoteRequired":"MAJORITY"},"introducedDate":"2025-01-07","issueTags":[{"matchCount":null,"matchedField":"official subject line","matchedText":"Recall elections: successors.","ruleId":"tag-direct-3","tag":"direct-democracy"}],"judicialNote":"Leg